\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Balanced accuracy across all three heterogeneity levels: near-IID ($\alpha {=}1.0$, $K{=}3$), moderate ($\alpha {=}0.5$, $K{=}5$) and severe ($\alpha {=}0.1$, $K{=}5$). The severe panel additionally shows the no-decomposition baseline. The centralized upper bound is shown for reference. At $\alpha {=}0.1$ the no-decomposition baseline sits below FedSA-Drift{} on this validation curve (0.4460 against 0.5196 at round 25), yet on the test split their mean per-client balanced accuracies are within one point (0.3778 against 0.3681) while the baseline records zero melanoma recall throughout. Balanced accuracy alone therefore does not distinguish the two.}}{39}{figure.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison_bal_acc}{{14}{39}{Balanced accuracy across all three heterogeneity levels: near-IID ($\alpha {=}1.0$, $K{=}3$), moderate ($\alpha {=}0.5$, $K{=}5$) and severe ($\alpha {=}0.1$, $K{=}5$). The severe panel additionally shows the no-decomposition baseline. The centralized upper bound is shown for reference. At $\alpha {=}0.1$ the no-decomposition baseline sits below \sadrift {} on this validation curve (0.4460 against 0.5196 at round 25), yet on the test split their mean per-client balanced accuracies are within one point (0.3778 against 0.3681) while the baseline records zero melanoma recall throughout. Balanced accuracy alone therefore does not distinguish the two}{figure.caption.19}{}}
